WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
A 77 gigahertz radar needs gain that plain CMOS cannot give,

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.800
and converters that must stay quiet

3
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.680
So SKU-7 splits the work across two dies.

4
00:00:12.682 --> 00:00:14.782
It starts with a frequency ramp.

5
00:00:15.322 --> 00:00:18.322
If that ramp bends anywhere across its sweep,

6
00:00:18.322 --> 00:00:21.762
the radar starts seeing targets that are not there.

7
00:00:21.793 --> 00:00:25.593
The oscillator runs at half the carrier, and a doubler lifts it

8
00:00:25.593 --> 00:00:27.673
to 77 gigahertz.

9
00:00:27.673 --> 00:00:31.393
The same chirp then serves transmit and receive.

10
00:00:31.414 --> 00:00:33.774
Four receivers mix each echo down.

11
00:00:34.314 --> 00:00:38.374
Frequency tells you distance, and the phase across the four tells you

12
00:00:38.374 --> 00:00:39.354
direction.

13
00:00:39.354 --> 00:00:42.336
The signal crosses between the dies exactly once,

14
00:00:42.336 --> 00:00:46.796
as four channels, and becomes a map of range against speed.

15
00:00:46.796 --> 00:00:50.655
Detection then holds a constant false-alarm rate against clutter,

16
00:00:50.655 --> 00:00:53.955
and beamforming adds direction to every target.

17
00:00:53.955 --> 00:00:58.262
The vehicle gets a list of up to 64 tracked targets,

18
00:00:58.262 --> 00:01:01.622
never the raw radar samples behind them.

19
00:01:01.622 --> 00:01:06.486
SKU-7 is pre-silicon, and its front end can only be proven on

20
00:01:06.486 --> 00:01:07.306
silicon.

21
00:01:07.826 --> 00:01:10.506
There is no FPGA stand-in

22
00:01:11.486 --> 00:01:13.806
Bring us your field of view and range.

23
00:01:14.366 --> 00:01:17.586
We will tell you what SKU-7 must prove.
